Package: GAModelSelection
Type: Package
Title: GA-Based Model Selection for Mixed Models
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R: c(person("David Pablo", "Quevedo Colmena", email = "davquecol@outlook.es", role = c("aut", "cre"), comment = "Researcher in CSIC (Spanish Research Council)"))
Description: Implements a genetic algorithm-based approach for automatic model selection in mixed 
 models. The package provides functions for automated data preprocessing (handling missing values, 
 normalizing the dependent variable, and outlier detection and management), candidate generation 
 (employing a comprehensive genetic map with genes for model approach (lmer, glmer, glmer.nb, glmmTMB), 
 family specification (binomial, gaussian, Gamma, poisson, nbinom2, nbinom1, compois, tweedie, 
 beta_family, betabinomial, inverse.gaussian, truncated_compois, truncated_poisson, ziGamma, 
 t_family, ordbeta), outlier removal (logical flag and removal percentage ranges), zero-inflation 
 handling (different formula configurations), normalization approach (no_transform, arcsinh_x, boxcox, 
 log_x, double_reverse_log, sqrt_x, yeojohnson, Lambert), shift addition and shift ranges, and link 
 function (log, logit, probit, inverse, cloglog, identity, sqrt)). It also includes additional control 
 genes for regularization (none, L1, L2, elastic_net), feature selection (all_predictors, stepwise, 
 penalized), GA stopping criterion (fixed_iterations, convergence, max_runtime), population 
 initialization (random, preselected), and cross-validation folds (5_fold, 10_fold, LOOCV). The package 
 builds models with fixed and random effects by integrating candidate configurations from the genetic 
 map, and evaluates model fitness using DHARMa diagnostic tests (KS, dispersion, outlier, heteroscedasticity) 
 to compute a fitness metric (product of p-values multiplied by the log-likelihood) along with additional 
 criteria such as AICc and cross-validation. Documentation Sections: Overview: Provides a comprehensive 
 introduction to the package, its theoretical foundations, and its intended applications in ecological and 
 scientific research. Installation: Contains detailed instructions on system prerequisites, installing 
 dependencies, and installing the package from GitHub or locally. Usage: Offers practical examples and a 
 demo function that illustrate the full workflow from data preprocessing through candidate generation, 
 model building, and fitness evaluation. Reference: Includes full, auto-generated documentation for each 
 function with detailed parameter descriptions, input formats, return values, and examples. Vignettes: 
 Provides extended tutorials and case studies to demonstrate real-world applications and advanced workflows. 
 News: Documents change logs, updates, and release notes.
License: CSIC
